# IoT Smart Monitoring System — Frontend Cursor Rules

# Stack: Angular 21 · Angular Material · SCSS · TypeScript strict mode

# Sprint structure: 6 sprints, 2 weeks each, rotating roles

# Current sprint: Sprint 1 — Authentication & User Profile

---

## Project Context

This is the frontend for a Smart IoT Monitoring System built for a DXC/AAST internship.
The app monitors sensor data (traffic, air pollution, street lighting) in real time.
Sprint 1 scope: Sign-Up, Sign-In, and Profile Page only.
Backend: Java Spring Boot REST API (not yet available — mock with JSON Server during dev).
Database: MySQL (handled entirely by backend, not your concern).

---

## Folder Structure

Strictly follow this structure. Never generate files outside of it without asking:

src/
app/
core/
services/ ← all HTTP services live here (AuthService, UserService)
guards/ ← route guards live here (AuthGuard)
interceptors/ ← HTTP interceptors live here (AuthInterceptor)
models/ ← TypeScript interfaces live here (User, LoginRequest, etc.)
features/
auth/
login/ ← LoginComponent
signup/ ← SignupComponent
home/ ← HomeComponent (stub — just welcome screen)
profile/ ← ProfileComponent
shared/
components/ ← reusable UI components (loading spinner, error message, etc.)
environments/
environment.ts ← dev config (points to mock backend)
environment.prod.ts ← prod config (points to real backend)

---

## Angular Conventions — Always Follow These

### Forms

- ALWAYS use Reactive Forms (FormGroup, FormControl, Validators) — never Template-driven forms
- ALWAYS define the form in the TypeScript class, not the HTML template
- ALWAYS show inline validation errors under each field
- Error messages must appear: on field blur OR on failed submit attempt
- Never disable the submit button to indicate validation — show errors instead

### HTTP & Services

- NEVER make HTTP calls directly inside a component
- ALL HTTP calls go inside a service in core/services/
- ALWAYS handle both success and error cases for every HTTP call
- ALWAYS show a loading state (spinner or disabled button) while a request is in flight
- ALWAYS show a human-readable error message on failure — never just console.error()
- Use Angular's HttpClient — never fetch() or axios

### TypeScript

- Strict mode is ON — never use `any` type
- Always define interfaces for API request/response shapes in core/models/
- Use `readonly` for properties that should not be mutated

### Components

- Every component must have a single clear responsibility
- Use OnPush change detection for performance: changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush
- Unsubscribe from Observables in ngOnDestroy (use takeUntilDestroyed or a Subject)
- Never subscribe inside a subscribe (chain with operators instead)

### Routing

- All routes defined in app-routing.module.ts only
- /home and /profile are protected by AuthGuard
- / redirects to /login
- Never use Router.navigate() in a service — only in components

### Styling

- SCSS only — no inline styles, no style attributes in HTML
- Use Angular Material components for all form fields, buttons, and feedback
- Define color tokens and typography in a global \_variables.scss file
- Never hardcode hex colors in component SCSS files — always use variables or Material theming

---

## API Contract (agreed with backend team)

Base URL (dev): http://localhost:3000 ← JSON Server mock
Base URL (prod): http://localhost:8080/api ← Java Spring Boot (update when backend is ready)

### Endpoints

POST /auth/register
Request body: { email, firstName, lastName, password, profilePicture? }
Success: 201 { message: "User created successfully", userId }
Error 409: { message: "Email already exists" }
Error 500: { message: "Internal error occurred" }

POST /auth/login
Request body: { email, password }
Success: 200 { token, user: { id, email, firstName, lastName, profilePicture } }
Error 401: { message: "Invalid email or password" }

GET /users/profile
Headers: Authorization: Bearer <token>
Success: 200 { id, email, firstName, lastName, profilePicture }
Error 401: redirect to /login

PUT /users/profile/password
Headers: Authorization: Bearer <token>
Request body: { currentPassword, newPassword }
Success: 200 { message: "Password updated successfully" }
Error 400: { message: "Current password is incorrect" }

PUT /users/profile/picture
Headers: Authorization: Bearer <token>
Request body: { profilePicture } ← base64 string or URL (TBD with backend)
Success: 200 { message: "Profile picture updated", profilePicture }

---

## Auth Token Handling

- Store JWT token in localStorage under the key: 'iot_auth_token'
- Store user object in localStorage under the key: 'iot_user'
- AuthGuard checks for existence of 'iot_auth_token' — if missing, redirect to /login
- AuthInterceptor automatically attaches token to every outgoing request
- On 401 response from any endpoint: clear localStorage and redirect to /login

---

## Exception Handling Rules (Grading Rubric Critical)

Every API call MUST handle ALL of these cases — this is explicitly on the grading rubric:

1. Loading state: show spinner or disable button while request is in flight
2. Success: show success message or navigate, never just silently succeed
3. Client validation error (400): show the specific message from the API response
4. Auth error (401): clear token, redirect to login
5. Conflict error (409): show specific message (e.g., "This email is already registered")
6. Server error (500): show "An internal error occurred. Please try again."
7. Network error (no response): show "Could not connect to the server. Check your connection."

Never catch an error and do nothing. Never show only a console.error().

---

## What NOT to Generate

- No Docker files — that is DevOps responsibility
- No Postman collections — that is Tester 2 responsibility
- No backend Java code
- No database queries or schemas
- No unit test files unless explicitly asked
- No server-side rendering (SSR) code

---

## Code Style

- Single quotes for strings in TypeScript
- 2-space indentation
- Trailing commas in multi-line objects/arrays
- Explicit return types on all public methods
- Meaningful variable names — never a, b, x, temp
- Comments only for non-obvious logic — not for every line

---

## Cursor Behavior Instructions

When generating code for this project:

1. Always check the folder structure above and place files in the correct location
2. Always generate the TypeScript interface for any new data shape before generating the service method
3. Always include error handling in every service method — never a bare .subscribe()
4. When generating a component, always generate: the .ts file, .html file, and .scss file together
5. When asked to add a new API call, also update the environment files if a new endpoint is introduced
6. Never generate template-driven forms — always Reactive Forms
7. When generating form validation, always generate the corresponding error message display in the template
8. Prefer standalone components if using Angular 17+ standalone API — ask if unsure

---

## Commit Message Convention

Format: type(scope): description
Types: feat | fix | style | refactor | chore
Examples:
feat(auth): add sign-up form with validation
feat(auth): integrate register API call with error handling
fix(profile): handle 401 on profile picture update
chore(setup): initialize Angular project with routing and Material
